Chapter: SourceDefWire

  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The wire format of a source-definition record in the fact store, in one
 place. A definition is written by RepoProtocolPersist (apps) and read by
 the compiler through FactDisk (foreword); before this chapter each of
 them carried its own copy of the pipe layout, and a change to the format
 in one and not the other was a silent divergence a crossing test could
 catch only at runtime. The format now has a single definition, and both
 sides encode and decode through it.

 The record is nine pipe-delimited header fields followed by the content:

   hash|path|quire|chapter|signer-fp|sig-hex|author|timestamp|len|<content>

 None of the nine header fields can hold a pipe. The content can, and
 nearly always does -- a pipe is how the language writes a sum type -- so
 the content is the last field, it is length-prefixed, and it is taken
 verbatim after the ninth pipe, whatever bytes follow. A line with no
 ninth pipe is not in this format (it is one of the old pointer-shaped
 records that carried no content); decode returns None and leaves the
 caller to decide what an absent record means.

 The hex codec is spelled out here rather than cited from Encode/Hex or
 Parse. Those carry a `hex-digit-value` that collides, in the compiler's
 own compilation unit, with the compiler's function of the same name over
 a different argument type -- a collision that compiles fine anywhere else
 and breaks only the self-compile. A self-contained codec with private
 names cannot do that.

 We say:

Section: The Record

  SourceDefWire = record {
   sdw-hash : Text,
   sdw-path : Text,
   sdw-quire : Text,
   sdw-chapter : Text,
   sdw-signer : Text,
   sdw-signature : List Integer,
   sdw-author : Text,
   sdw-timestamp : Integer,
   sdw-content : Text
  }

Section: Hex

  sdw-hex-chars : Text = "0123456789abcdef"

  sdw-nibble : Integer -> Text
  sdw-nibble (n) = char-to-text (char-at sdw-hex-chars n)

  sdw-byte-hex : Integer -> Text
  sdw-byte-hex (b) = sdw-nibble (bit-shru b 4) & sdw-nibble (bit-and b 15)

  sdw-bytes-to-hex : List Integer -> Text
  sdw-bytes-to-hex (bs) = sdw-bth-loop bs 0 (list-length bs) ""

  sdw-bth-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  sdw-bth-loop (bs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else sdw-bth-loop bs (i + 1) len (acc & sdw-byte-hex (list-at bs i))

 CCE orders letters by frequency, not alphabetically, so a..f is not a
 range and the six letters are named one at a time; the codes are asked of
 the encoding rather than written down.

  sdw-hex-digit : Char -> Integer
  sdw-hex-digit (c) =
   let n = char-code c
   in if n == char-code (char-at "0" 0) then 0
   else if n == char-code (char-at "1" 0) then 1
   else if n == char-code (char-at "2" 0) then 2
   else if n == char-code (char-at "3" 0) then 3
   else if n == char-code (char-at "4" 0) then 4
   else if n == char-code (char-at "5" 0) then 5
   else if n == char-code (char-at "6" 0) then 6
   else if n == char-code (char-at "7" 0) then 7
   else if n == char-code (char-at "8" 0) then 8
   else if n == char-code (char-at "9" 0) then 9
   else if n == char-code (char-at "a" 0) then 10
   else if n == char-code (char-at "b" 0) then 11
   else if n == char-code (char-at "c" 0) then 12
   else if n == char-code (char-at "d" 0) then 13
   else if n == char-code (char-at "e" 0) then 14
   else if n == char-code (char-at "f" 0) then 15
   else 0

  sdw-hex-decode : Text -> List Integer
  sdw-hex-decode (s) = sdw-hex-loop s 0 (text-length s) []

  sdw-hex-loop : Text, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  sdw-hex-loop (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i + 1 >= len then acc
   else let hi = sdw-hex-digit (char-at s i)
   in let lo = sdw-hex-digit (char-at s (i + 1))
   in sdw-hex-loop s (i + 2) len (list-push acc (bit-or (bit-shl hi 4) lo))

Section: Finding The Ninth Pipe

  sdw-pipe-code : Integer = char-code (char-at "|" 0)

  sdw-nth-pipe : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sdw-nth-pipe (s) (n) (i) (seen) =
   if i >= text-length s then 0 - 1
   else if char-code (char-at s i) == sdw-pipe-code then
    (if seen + 1 == n then i else sdw-nth-pipe s n (i + 1) (seen + 1))
   else sdw-nth-pipe s n (i + 1) seen

Section: Encode

  sdw-encode : SourceDefWire -> Text
  sdw-encode (d) =
   let sig-hex = sdw-bytes-to-hex (d.sdw-signature)
   in d.sdw-hash & "|" & d.sdw-path & "|" & d.sdw-quire & "|" & d.sdw-chapter
   & "|" & d.sdw-signer & "|" & sig-hex
   & "|" & d.sdw-author & "|" & show (d.sdw-timestamp)
   & "|" & show (text-length (d.sdw-content)) & "|" & d.sdw-content

Section: Decode

 The ninth pipe ends the header; the content is the next `len` characters,
 taken whole. A header with fewer than nine fields, or a line with no
 ninth pipe, is not this format and decodes to None.

  sdw-decode : Text -> Maybe SourceDefWire
  sdw-decode (line) =
   let p9 = sdw-nth-pipe line 9 0 0
   in if p9 < 0 then None
   else let header = substring line 0 p9
   in let parts = text-split header "|"
   in if list-length parts < 9 then None
   else let clen = text-to-integer (list-at parts 8)
   in Just (SourceDefWire {
     sdw-hash = list-at parts 0,
     sdw-path = list-at parts 1,
     sdw-quire = list-at parts 2,
     sdw-chapter = list-at parts 3,
     sdw-signer = list-at parts 4,
     sdw-signature = sdw-hex-decode (list-at parts 5),
     sdw-author = list-at parts 6,
     sdw-timestamp = text-to-integer (list-at parts 7),
     sdw-content = substring line (p9 + 1) clen
   })
